#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093390 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n$$\n0 \\le x < 2π\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation contains a transformed sine function.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the elementary equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{X} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\frac{π}{3} + 2nπ, \\mid X|= \\frac{2π}{3} + 2nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is assumed to be any integer.\n\nTo find the general solution of the transformed sine equation, we replace $X$ with the sine function's input, which is\n\n$$\nx-\\dfrac{\\pi}3\n$$\n\nin this case. This gives the general solution\n\n$$\nx - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi, \\qquad\\qquad x - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above two equations for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{2π}{3} + 2nπ\\begin{vmatrix}x|= π + 2nπ \\\\ x|= \\frac{2π}{3} + \\frac{6nπ}{3}\\end{vmatrix}x|= (1 + 2n)π \\\\\nx &= (2π(1 + 3n))/(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\nn=0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nuntil we have generated all solutions in the domain\n\n$$\n0\\leq x <2\\pi\n$$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\dfrac{2\\pi}3$ and $x=\\pi$.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain\n\n$$\n0\\leq x < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad x_2 = \\pi\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d59e641-96b3-527c-98e2-41c5def454e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2683 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
